# ABK SoC Power Optimization
#
config ABK_SOC_OPT
	tristate "ABK SoC power optimization"
	depends on CPU_FREQ
	default m
	help
	  Kernel-side cpufreq capping module.
	  Uses a cpufreq policy notifier to enforce per-cluster
	  frequency limits, preventing userspace daemons (perfd,
	  thermal-engine) from overriding them.

	  Sysfs control at /sys/kernel/abk_soc_opt/
	  Works with the AppOpt userspace tool.

	  NOTE ON BUILD MODE:
	  Prefer M (loadable module) over Y (builtin).

	  Built with =y the driver runs at device_initcall time, which on
	  Qualcomm platforms is *earlier* than qcom-cpufreq-hw finishes
	  probing. cpufreq_cpu_get() then returns NULL for every CPU, the
	  cluster scan records num_clusters=0, and the module stays
	  permanently inert: the sysfs nodes exist and accept writes, but
	  nothing is ever enforced. It cannot be recovered by reloading,
	  because a builtin module cannot be unloaded.

	  Built with =m the initcall ordering is naturally correct and the
	  module can be reloaded at will. If you must build it in (=y), the
	  driver now retries the scan and also rescans on the first
	  CPUFREQ_CREATE_POLICY notification, so it should recover on its
	  own -- but =m is still the safer choice.

	  To compile as a module, choose M here.
